-- Task 3: the most items we can buy with $100 (TASK-03.md).
-- Walk-through and proof: how-it-works.md. Measurements on a large table: sql-performance.md.
--
-- Strategy: every item counts as one, whatever it costs, so each dollar goes to the cheapest
-- item still in stock. Buy products in price order until the money runs out.
--
-- Performance: the answer depends only on the cheapest few products, so read only those.
--   * Every price is at least $1, so $100 can pay for at most 100 products.
--     The 101st cheapest product onwards can never get any money.
--   * A product priced over $100 can never be bought.
-- So rather than sort the whole table, ask for the 100 cheapest products we can afford.
-- Postgres then keeps only 100 rows in memory while it scans (a "top-N heapsort"), and with an
-- index on (price, name) it reads the first 100 index entries and stops, however big the table.
WITH cheapest_affordable_products AS (
    SELECT name, price, quantity
    FROM products
    WHERE price <= 100       -- over budget on its own: can never be bought
    ORDER BY price, name     -- cheapest first; name breaks ties so the order is the same every run
    LIMIT 100                -- $100 budget / $1 lowest possible price = most products that can get money
),

-- How much money is left when we reach each product, if every cheaper product was bought in full.
-- This step sorts again, but only the 100 rows above, which costs nothing.
money_left_per_product AS (
    SELECT price,
           quantity,
           -- Running total (window function): cost of this product and every cheaper one, in full.
           -- Subtract this product's own cost to get what was spent before it.
           100 - (sum(price * quantity) OVER (ORDER BY price, name) - price * quantity) AS money_left
    FROM cheapest_affordable_products
),

-- Buy as many as the money left allows, but no more than are in stock.
-- money_left goes negative once the budget is spent; greatest() turns that into "buy none".
-- Integer division rounds down, which is what we want: we can't buy part of an item.
items_bought_per_product AS (
    SELECT least(quantity, greatest(0, money_left) / price) AS items_bought
    FROM money_left_per_product
)

-- sum() of no rows (an empty table) is NULL, so coalesce() makes it 0.
SELECT coalesce(sum(items_bought), 0) AS quantity
FROM items_bought_per_product;
